    <section>
      <h2>Overview</h2>
      <p>
        This article examines dissociation and depersonalization
        through the lens of contemporary alienation, proposing
        the concept of <em>double expropriation</em> to describe
        a condition in which individuals become estranged not
        only from their social and material existence, but also
        from their own subjective experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        It introduces the notion of <em>third-person subjectivity</em>
        to explore the relationship between dissociative
        experience, identity, and the structural transformations
        of late capitalism.
      </p>
